@@ -0,0 +1,23 @@
+Satisfying ground witness:
+  VS = vCons(1, .ValSeq)
+  allNonNegativeInts(VS) = true
+  canonical.py sort_array([1]) = [1]
+  solution.py sort_array([1]) = [1]
+  fixed K execution allocates the inner sorted list at heap 0, allocates the
+  outer keyed-sorted list at heap 1, and returns ref(1).
+
+The fresh mutation requires the same heap transition but changes the returned
+value to ref(0). Therefore it is false for this satisfying input (and in fact
+for every satisfying input), while still parsing and building successfully.
+
+Stronger content-level witness:
+  VS = vCons(3, vCons(4, .ValSeq))
+  allNonNegativeInts(VS) = true
+  decimal pre-sort sortVS(VS) denotes [3, 4]
+  canonical.py sort_array([3, 4]) = [4, 3]
+  solution.py sort_array([3, 4]) = [4, 3]
+
+The content mutation keeps ref(1) but requires heap 1 to contain only
+sortVS(VS), dropping sortKeyVS. It is therefore false on [3, 4]. Its dry run
+exits 0, and its proof exits 1 with an implication residual comparing
+sortKeyVS(sortVS(VS), popcountKeyClosure) against sortVS(VS).
